WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.410
- So if you've made a mistake,
something isn't working,

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.690
stop throwing good money
after bad, just move on.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.880
Learn the lesson that
you're supposed to learn

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.713
and then apply it.

00:00:09.713 --> 00:00:11.120
You can always make mistakes,

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.053
but don't make the same mistake twice.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.140
- [Gary] You've got your perspective.

00:00:20.358 --> 00:00:21.191
I just want to be happy.

00:00:21.191 --> 00:00:22.090
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.590
- She's won more awards
than we can mention.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.190
We'd be here all day.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.273
Welcome Stephanie.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.163
- Thank you.

00:00:31.163 --> 00:00:31.996
Can you hear me?

00:00:31.996 --> 00:00:33.050
Am I on?
- We can, Steph.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.270
- You are super on.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.482
How are you?

00:00:35.482 --> 00:00:36.315
- I'm good.

00:00:36.315 --> 00:00:37.260
How are you, Gary?

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.170
Thank you, Andrea, for that introduction.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.100
That was pretty spectacular.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.940
It sounds like something
w'd do in the ring.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:44.773
Am I blushing?

00:00:44.773 --> 00:00:46.240
You can see I'm blushing.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.220
- Not yet, but one
thing to make you blush,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.830
as a hardcore WWE fan for my whole life,

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.438
as Andrea was doing the intro,

00:00:55.438 --> 00:00:57.210
talking about leadership,

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.080
a lot of times people,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.390
whether it's Me Too, Black Lives Matter,

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.010
they react after the fact,

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.970
and then there's leadership that reacts

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.840
prior to when they're supposed to,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.629
or when the lights are on.

00:01:06.629 --> 00:01:09.830
As Andrea was introing you,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.830
I was smiling because the female
aspect of the WWE universe

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.620
is so advanced at this point

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.690
and I want you to talk about it.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.240
But what I was thinking
in my mind is like,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.140
that is so true because
when I was growing up,

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.170
Wendi Richter and the Fabulous
Moolah got some spotlight,

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.640
but there just wasn't that much else.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.080
And to watch what's
transformed in the organization

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.170
over the last two decades on this front,

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.118
and even the way women were positioned

00:01:36.118 --> 00:01:36.951
and things of that nature,

00:01:36.951 --> 00:01:38.520
it's just incredible what's happened.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.861
Please elaborate.

00:01:39.861 --> 00:01:40.800
- Absolutely.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.300
Well, it really started

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.520
with how we started to train and recruit

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.490
our female athletes.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.970
We wanted the most elite athletes

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.160
from both a male and female standpoint,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.920
and we started training
them the same as the men

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.630
and giving them the same opportunities,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.120
the same match time,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.290
which like anything else,

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.740
the more reps you have, the
better you're gonna get.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.770
And what happened is at NXT,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.220
which started off as
our developmental brand

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.660
and evolved into our third global brand,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.450
the women started really stealing the show

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.760
and there were chants

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.090
of this is wrestling
and women's wrestling.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.633
- [Gary] I remember.

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:16.135
- So then on Ron's SmackDown,

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:18.620
and this was actually February 2015,

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.540
we had a women's tag match,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.450
which lasted all of 30
seconds in a three hour show,

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.800
which was unfortunately the norm,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.610
and our audience started a hashtag

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.240
called give divas a chance

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.950
that trended worldwide for three days

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.210
demanding more screen time,

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.370
better athleticism, better storylines,

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.610
better character
development for our women.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.520
And our chairman and CEO, my father,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.430
who you know well Gary,

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.007
responded at the highest level and said,

00:02:46.007 --> 00:02:48.457
"We hear you, keep watching,
# givedivasachance."

00:02:50.405 --> 00:02:51.490
- Steph, I'm really curious.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.940
We have some really interesting parallels

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.430
with growing up in a family business,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.500
just on my own curiosity,

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.130
just knowing there's a lot
of different people watching

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.840
that are in family businesses,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.430
your dad, such an icon,

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.503
your grandfather such an icon,

00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:08.670
maybe for the people that
are watching right now,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.400
'cause there's a mix of
entrepreneurs, executives,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.550
there's such a mix on this show,

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.560
which kudos to Andrea and the team

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.060
for creating this environment,

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:18.400
when did your dad start listening to you?

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.540
It's really as simple as that,

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.430
like knowing your dad is such a force,

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.721
like how did you go navigate that?

00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:28.790
Like my dad actually gave me a lot of room

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.100
'cause I got in at 14
and really proved myself.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.290
And even when he was
giving me a lot of room,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.150
any room he didn't give me
for something that I wanted

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.440
was a frustrating point,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.850
it's family dynamics.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.210
How did that evolve for you

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.460
where your voice became
bigger at the table?

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:44.450
How was that process for you?

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.100
Because I think within that story,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.560
and obviously so many
people know the two of you,

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.660
it might help somebody who's
going through it right now,

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.980
where they just can't get their parent

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.403
to give them that look.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.071
- I think I'm still fighting
for it, honestly, Gary.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:00.746
It's not like I have a
bigger voice at the table,

00:04:00.746 --> 00:04:01.579
that's for sure.

00:04:01.579 --> 00:04:02.412
- But I would assume, Steph,

00:04:02.412 --> 00:04:03.245
and I'm sorry to interrupt,

00:04:03.245 --> 00:04:05.260
it's probably bigger than it
was five or seven years ago.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:09.570
To your point, like my dad
still also in all my accolades

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.070
pushes against some of my ideas.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.650
I respect that, I understand that,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.290
but I would assume it
is a little bit bigger

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.100
than it used to be, no?

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.380
- Yeah, for sure

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.210
and I think it ebbs and
flows and it's complicated.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.260
Family businesses is super complicated,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.860
especially when you have
such a strong personality.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.850
As you were mentioning before

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.050
in your own business with you as the boss,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.630
when your boss has such
a strong personality

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.360
and is such an icon,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.740
my dad grew up in a trailer park

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.020
and he truly lived the American dream

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:44.420
and took what was in Northeast
regional territorial business

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.190
and turned it into this global phenomenon

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.090
in 108 countries and 28 languages.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.810
We have the WWE Network,

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.877
one of the first to launch an OTT service

00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:55.360
behind Netflix and Hulu

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.330
because he took a calculated risk

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.760
betting on himself and his business,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.930
fourth most viewed YouTube
channel in the world,

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.260
over a billion social media followers,

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:05.940
that's because of him and his vision.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.525
And it takes someone strong
who makes quick decisions,

00:05:09.525 --> 00:05:12.278
to harken back to your
previous conversation,

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:13.970
and who's just truly driven,

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:15.493
but who also listens.

00:05:15.493 --> 00:05:17.817
- 100%.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:20.830
I'm sorry to interrupt,
but I just want to,

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.640
I think praise is always
fun when you get to hear it,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.370
literally, because I
realized somewhere in my 30's

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:29.397
that I had the knack of
understanding where attention was,

00:05:29.397 --> 00:05:33.660
there are very few people
in the history of business

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.140
that understood attention
better than your father.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.970
Whether it was Saturday morning,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.220
whether it was cartoons,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.320
whether it was Saturday
night's main event,

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.260
whether it was MTV,

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.260
whether it was Cindy Lauper or Liberace,

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:48.730
it's so intuitive to me,

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:51.060
it's how I built my universe,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.850
like I would argue that your father

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:57.280
as an executive from afar
analyzing almost everybody

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:58.680
is one of the great listeners,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:05:59.730
even though when he talks,

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:03.170
he talks with conviction and
I'm sure very much juice,

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.470
again, knowing family businesses,

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:05.890
but it's funny you said that,

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:08.580
I actually put your dad's listening skills

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:11.600
at the height of modern culture.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:12.433
- Agreed.

00:06:12.433 --> 00:06:14.410
And he doesn't just listen to,

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:15.560
he listens to fans,

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:16.860
he listens to the live audience,

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.620
he listens on social media,

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.680
but he also listens to our employees.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.730
And he's always said,

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.500
you never know where a great
idea is gonna come from.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.317
So he solicits that opinion.

00:06:27.317 --> 00:06:29.620
He talks to people, he really does.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:30.870
- Speaking of leadership,

00:06:32.054 --> 00:06:32.960
do you feel that your father,

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:33.793
do you feel you,

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:34.626
do you feel that your husband,

00:06:34.626 --> 00:06:36.660
are good at admitting they're wrong?

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.630
I've been thinking a lot about listeners,

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:43.000
listeners actually stunningly
don't mind being wrong.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.330
How does that play out
in your leadership style

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.780
or the other leaders?

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.990
- So for me personally,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:52.339
I am very transparent and I own mistakes

00:06:52.339 --> 00:06:54.370
and I think that that's really important.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:55.784
I think that,

00:06:55.784 --> 00:06:59.040
there's a difference between being wrong

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.530
and making mistakes.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.970
And I know that that's
a subtlety in semantics,

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:06.289
but I do think human
psychology plays into that.

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:10.040
And again, just going back to Vince,

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.790
he believes in sunk cost.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.580
So if you've made a mistake,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:14.900
something isn't working,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.660
stop throwing good money after bad,

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:17.788
just move on.

00:07:17.788 --> 00:07:20.360
Learn the lesson that
you're supposed to learn

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:21.193
and then apply it.

00:07:21.193 --> 00:07:22.610
You can always make mistakes,

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.380
but don't make the same mistake twice.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.430
- Steph on that, please.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:28.730
- [Steph] Never tolerated.

00:07:30.864 --> 00:07:33.020
- Actually now, Andrea,

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.850
I'm getting extremely selfish here,

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:36.410
everybody's who's watching, I'm sorry,

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:37.690
I'm just nerding out too much.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.270
Steph, genuinely back to like learning

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:41.830
and watching audience,

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:43.380
let's give somebody a huge shout out.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.903
In your career watching the talent,

00:07:47.025 --> 00:07:49.160
who most pleasantly surprised you

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.130
from the raw human they were

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.170
when they walked in and
started doing undercards

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:54.680
to the superstar they became?

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.130
Who are you most impressed with

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:57.420
where you're like, oh my gosh,

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.560
I'm so proud of X or Y,

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.330
they came here as a
young kid or bushy eyed,

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:05.390
or maybe even came from a different league

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:06.970
and had too much ego,

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:11.970
and then you watched them
adjust and become more humble.

00:08:12.472 --> 00:08:14.561
Who really, really as a talent,

00:08:14.561 --> 00:08:16.850
did you see listened,

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:21.850
whether it was from your
dad, you, or the audience,

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.750
who listened the most in
the last decade or two

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:27.236
that you really were incredible with,

00:08:27.236 --> 00:08:28.069
like impressed with?

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:29.440
- I'm not sure they listened to the most.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.520
I mean there's so many stories
I could tell throughout time.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.280
I mean even Stone Cold Steve Austin,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.630
who was the ringmaster

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:38.270
and he was standing at Burton
and he tells this story

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:40.270
of he was watching somebody in the ring

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:41.640
and I'm not sure who it was,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.023
and he looked over it
at Vince and he said,

00:08:44.897 --> 00:08:45.990
"Hey, you should really
give that kid a push.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:47.790
They really got something."

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.867
And, you know, my dad
looked back and he said,

00:08:49.867 --> 00:08:51.900
"Well, all I can provide
is the opportunity.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.580
The rest is up to him."

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:54.490
And Austin said,

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.910
that's when the light
bulb went off for him,

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.910
like, oh, this is really about me

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.480
and my ability to listen and
my ability to grow my brand.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.540
And I think the talent that we have today

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.730
have more of an opportunity
than anybody ever did before

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.440
because of social media,

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.100
'cause they recognize the opportunity

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.450
to build their own brand
and their own voice,

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:16.770
both in and outside of the ring,

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:18.710
'cause the more people
are engaged with you

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:20.210
as a person, as an individual,

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.540
which again, I think is an evolution,

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.340
the more they're gonna
want to see you perform,

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.840
or they're gonna want to hear you talk

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.620
or see you in the ring.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.650
But I also think, Becky Lynch is a huge,

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.620
I could name so many, Drew McIntyre.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.901
I mean, he was here and then he left.

00:09:36.901 --> 00:09:41.901
He has truly learned so much
and matured as an individual

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.130
to now come back and be champion.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.310
- Let's finish with this.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:47.143
So many businesses,

00:09:47.143 --> 00:09:50.370
big and small pivoting during COVID.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:51.530
Just watching, again,

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:53.620
'cause I have so much
more knowledge as a fan,

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.920
the pivoting that the
organization has done,

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.100
what have you learned about this 150 days

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:02.600
about your organization or
about changing your mind

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.810
or pivoting or adjusting
or never saying never?

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.500
What's the biggest learning point?

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.960
Because obviously the way
you're producing today

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.730
is so different than ever before.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:12.760
What's the biggest insight

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:14.823
that you can learn from the macro?

00:10:15.979 --> 00:10:20.030
- Well, I think for WWE,
we never went off the air.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.970
So we've continued to produce
seven hours of content

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.850
every single week at a minimum
for our television partners,

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.020
for USA Network and Fox,

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.590
and including our pay-per-views.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:33.130
WrestleMania went from being
at Raymond James Stadium

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:35.570
and what was supposed to be
in front of 80,000 people

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.400
to being performed in
our performance center,

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.310
which is essentially a warehouse.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.070
And we took it to two days over one

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.850
with absolutely no fans in the audience.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:46.542
I think the ability to be fluid,

00:10:46.542 --> 00:10:50.660
the ability to make changes quickly,

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.290
the ability to listen and
then sticking to your mission

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:55.960
and reimagining your business,

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.990
but staying true to what your mission is.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:00.800
And WWE's mission is to put
smiles on people's faces

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:01.830
the world over.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.740
It's something that we
take very seriously.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.740
We've considered a responsibility

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.330
to stay on the air to entertain our fans

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:09.660
in the safest way possible,

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.530
as long as we can be safe

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:14.430
for our crew, employees and performers.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.746
- I love that.

00:11:15.746 --> 00:11:17.760
Steph, just another fun question.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.800
How about you as a human,

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:22.810
between either e-commerce or
your own media consumption,

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.340
I think all of us,

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:27.150
our behavior has changed during this time.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:30.700
Is there any product you now
buy online that's delivered

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.100
that you never considered pre COVID

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:34.380
and you're in love with the experience

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.290
or is there some show on Netflix or Hulu

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:39.940
or some app that you're playing with,

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:41.990
just you, the human being,

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:43.970
what's a new behavior,
a consumer behavior,

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:48.160
that you can feel that you've
established during this time?

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.430
- I've definitely transitioned
more to digital consumption.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.620
I think that you can
look at all of the trends

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:55.670
and everyone always assumed
it was going to go that way,

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:58.720
but I think it's pivoted
three to five times faster

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.440
than would have happened without COVID.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.060
And I'm certainly a
part of that transition.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:07.260
When I do watch programming
on linear television now,

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.590
I'm like, ugh, commercials,

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:11.219
like where did they come from?

00:12:11.219 --> 00:12:12.820
And that's not to say anything

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.100
against our advertising partners.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.210
I think it's going to
force the ad industry

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.430
really to partner with content
creators in a different way

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.170
to bring an experience

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:24.790
to the viewers or watchers
or fans or consumers,

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:25.680
whatever it is,

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.840
that is additive to them

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.420
versus taking away the
time of their viewing.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:32.633
It's gonna cause us all
to be more creative.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:34.423
- I agree.

00:12:34.423 --> 00:12:35.370
I appreciate your time.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.620
I hope you have a
wonderful rest of the year

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.470
and I can't wait to see you soon.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:41.303
- Thank you, Gary.

00:12:41.303 --> 00:12:42.240
I can't wait to see you too.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.633
And Andrea, I called
you Andrea, it's Andrea.

00:12:45.777 --> 00:12:48.630
- She answers to all
of them, all versions.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:49.800
She's great like that.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:50.633
- I'm from the Midwest,

00:12:50.633 --> 00:12:51.920
so it's really Andrea, Stephanie.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:53.610
- Okay, okay.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:56.160
But one last thing, Gary,
back on the women's evolution,

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.740
it ultimately wound up

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.540
with our women headlining and
main eventing WrestleMania,

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:03.200
which is akin to our Super
Bowl or the World Cup

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:05.880
and also having not one
but two women's matches

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.030
in Saudi Arabia over the past two years.

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:09.930
So I think that's pretty strong.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:10.763
- Good for you.

00:13:10.763 --> 00:13:11.660
Thank you so much.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:12.493
Hey, everybody on YouTube.

00:13:12.493 --> 00:13:14.903
First of all, thank you so
much, so humbled for your time.

00:13:14.903 --> 00:13:17.720
I don't wear a watch, but
time is the biggest asset.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:19.050
So thank you for watching that video.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.370
If you got some value out of that,

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:23.280
there's plenty more where that came from.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:26.493
Feel free to check it out.

